# boichard_fig2.ped -- PUBLISHED WORKED EXAMPLE, transcribed with provenance.
#
# SOURCE: Boichard D, Maignel L, Verrier E (1997) "The value of using
#         probabilities of gene origin to measure genetic variability in a
#         population." Genet Sel Evol 29:5-23.
#         FIGURE 2 and TABLE II, article pages 11-12.
#         (HAL scan hal-00894153; PDF page 9 = article page 12.)
#
# All 20 animals carry the paper's own numbering.
#
# TOPOLOGY, read from the figure (bar convention; left parent recorded as sire,
# right as dam -- the paper marks no sexes and no published value depends on it):
#
#   Family 1:  1 x 2 -> 5
#              3 x 4 -> 6
#              5 x 6 -> 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14
#
#   Family 2:  15 x 16 -> 17, 18
#              17 x 18 -> 19, 20
#
#   founders: 1, 2, 3, 4 (family 1) and 15, 16 (family 2) -- six in total,
#   which is the "Total No of founders" column of Table II.
#
#   population under study: {7..14} u {19, 20} -- ten animals, drawn inside
#   the ellipse. Family 1 contributes 8 of them, family 2 contributes 2.
#
# PUBLISHED EXPECTED VALUES (Table II)
#
#     Population   founders   f_e    f_a    N_g
#     Total            6      5.6    2.94   2.5
#     Family 1         4      4.0    2.0    1.8
#     Family 2         2      2.0    2.0    1.1
#
#   f_e and f_a are deterministic and are the targets for the Appendix A and
#   Appendix B oracles. N_g is a Monte-Carlo gene-drop figure and is NOT an
#   exact target.
#
# CORROBORATION -- this is not a guessed topology. Hand-derivation from
# Appendices A and B reproduces all six deterministic values:
#
#     Total     f_e = 1/(4*0.2^2 + 2*0.1^2)          = 5.556  (published 5.6)
#               f_a = 1/(0.4^2+0.4^2+0.1^2+0.1^2)    = 2.941  (published 2.94)
#     Family 1  f_e = 4.0, f_a = 1/(0.5^2+0.5^2)     = 2.0    (published 4.0, 2.0)
#     Family 2  f_e = 2.0, f_a = 2.0                          (published 2.0, 2.0)
#
#   The family figures require restricting the reference population to that
#   family's offspring; the PyPedal-free oracle takes the reference set
#   explicitly, so one fixture serves all three rows.
#
# GENERATIONS are labels designating the reference population, not biological
# depth: 1 = ancestors, 2 = the population under study. Note that this puts 17
# and 18 in generation 1 even though they are offspring of 15 x 16 -- they are
# ancestors of the population, not members of it, which is exactly the
# distinction the label encodes.
#
# pedformat: asdg
# Columns are:
#    Animal Sire Dam Generation
1 0 0 1
2 0 0 1
3 0 0 1
4 0 0 1
5 1 2 1
6 3 4 1
7 5 6 2
8 5 6 2
9 5 6 2
10 5 6 2
11 5 6 2
12 5 6 2
13 5 6 2
14 5 6 2
15 0 0 1
16 0 0 1
17 15 16 1
18 15 16 1
19 17 18 2
20 17 18 2
